# Đề bài

Cho $a$, $b$ là các số dương và thỏa mãn hệ thức $\dfrac{\sin^4x}{a}+\dfrac{\cos^4x}{b}=\dfrac{1}{a+b}$. Chứng minh rằng $\dfrac{\sin^{2022}x}{a^{1010}}+\dfrac{\cos^{2022}x}{b^{1010}}=\dfrac{1}{(a+b)^{1010}}.$

# Lời giải

Ta có
$$
\begin{aligned}
\dfrac{\sin^4x}{a}+\dfrac{\cos^4x}{b}=\dfrac{1}{a+b}&\Leftrightarrow (a+b)\left(\dfrac{\sin^4x}{a}+\dfrac{\cos^4x}{b}\right)=1\\
& \Leftrightarrow (a+b)\left(\dfrac{\sin^4x}{a}+\dfrac{\cos^4x}{b}\right)=\left(\sin^2x+\cos^2x\right)^2\\
& \Leftrightarrow \dfrac{a}{b}\cos^4x+\dfrac{b}{a}\sin^4x-2\sin^2x\cos^2x=0\\
& \Leftrightarrow \left(\sqrt{\dfrac{b}{a}}\cos^2x-\sqrt{\dfrac{a}{b}}\sin^2x\right)^2=0\\
& \Leftrightarrow \sqrt{\dfrac{b}{a}}\cos^2x=\sqrt{\dfrac{a}{b}}\sin^2x\\
& \Leftrightarrow \dfrac{\sin^2x}{a}=\dfrac{\cos^2x}{b}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Từ đó suy ra $\dfrac{\sin^2x}{a}=\dfrac{\cos^2x}{b}=\dfrac{1}{a+b}>0$.

Đặt $t=\dfrac{1}{a+b}\Rightarrow\left\{\begin{aligned}&\sin^2x=at\\&\cos^2x=bt\end{aligned}\right.$, do đó ta có $\left\{\begin{aligned}&\sin^{2022}x=a^{1011}t^{1011}\\&\cos^{2022}x=b^{1011}t^{1011}.\end{aligned}\right.$

Vậy $\dfrac{\sin^{2022}x}{a^{1010}}+\dfrac{\cos^{2022}x}{b^{1010}}=\dfrac{a^{1011}t^{1011}}{a^{1010}}+\dfrac{b^{1011}t^{1011}}{b^{1010}}=(a+b)t^{1011}=\dfrac{1}{(a+b)^{1010}}.$

